---
description: When physics is uncertain, bias models against energy closure — never optimistic.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Conservative physics

If a number is not measured or not in a datasheet range, implement the **pessimistic** side. A design must not close because the model was kind.

- **Prop maps:** if the (speed, RPM) point is on the manufacturer map, use that T and P (density-scaled). Use C_T(J) similarity only as a check, or to fill a gap that is still below windmill J. Flag if they disagree by more than `PROP_SANITY_REL`. Never n²-scale RPM down at fixed speed once the first map block that covers that speed is already above the required thrust — that invented J past zero-thrust.
- **ESC / motor:** do not keep 95% ESC efficiency below ~50% duty. Low-RPM cruise is not a free I0 win.
- **Aero:** do not take unmodeled credits (T-tail height reducing downwash, flight cooling, swirl) unless they hurt the metric you care about. Handbook downwash `2 CL/(π AR)` is already a coarse model — do not “improve” it in the direction that lightens tail download or drag without a cited method.
- **Mass / solar / battery:** heavier structure, less array, stricter charge limits, not the reverse.
- **FLAGGED** assumptions must be conservative. If you cannot tell which way an effect goes, pick the way that makes night bus higher or SOC worse, and say so.

Do not silently undo a conservative factor to help a candidate close.
